Give the echo canceller room for an echo that comes earlier

When the sound server misses its deadlines (PipeWire on a busy computer) it can leave out
some of what an application played or recorded around the missed cycles, and the echo then
reaches the microphone earlier, in samples, than the echo canceller's alignment. AEC3
places the echo 2 ms (delay_headroom_samples 32 at 16 kHz) after the start of its linear
filter, so an echo even a few ms earlier was out of the filter's reach, and the other
person's voice went back to them for a whole turn until the delay estimator found the echo
again, a second or more later, 2 to 8 blocks shorter. An echo that comes later (silence
played on an underflow) stays inside the filter.

The processing now asks AEC3 for 256 samples (4 blocks, 16 ms) of headroom and makes the
refined and coarse filters 4 blocks longer (17 blocks), so that they reach as far after the
echo as before; the same for the multichannel configuration.

Measured in the call lab (tools/call-lab/mic.sh echo, ECHO_STALL stopping the session's
PipeWire now and then, the echo's move timed in the samples each copy wrote and read):
- moves of the echo up to 16 ms earlier were followed by a turn let through 30 times in
  68 before, 2 in 66 after; larger moves 39 in 132 before, 24 in 131 after;
- with PipeWire's quantum left free (256 samples there, as on a desktop) and stalls of
  20 and 40 ms: 42 turns let through in 228 before, 15 in 231 after;
- with the quantum fixed at 480 and stalls of 40 to 300 ms, where the moves are mostly
  20 to 60 ms: 13 in 103 before, 16 in 102 after (no change);
- without stalls: none in 94, the echo check's figures as before (the other's voice sent
  on 74 dB under one's own at the median, 76 dB in a call's first turn), and every other
  microphone check of the lab passes.

